C++ half of WI-digaj. The keywords that decide C++ dispatch exist only
on the in-class declaration: an out-of-line definition (int
Square::area() {...}, usually in a .cc apart from the header) repeats
no virtual / override / static. So:

- cpp: class / struct symbols record meta virtual_members (declared
  virtual, override or final), override_members (override / final) and
  static_members, read from the class body; a member defined inline
  also carries its keywords as modifiers. Three new symbol_meta keys.
- type_hierarchy: cpp leaves NO_VIRTUAL_EXTENDS_LANGUAGES; a pair
  dispatches when the member is virtual in the parent or any ancestor
  (C++'s implicit virtual), or is the parent's pure virtual, or the
  child declares it override / final; never when static on either side.

On a header + .cc fixture: a concrete base's virtual reaches its
overrides, also through a non-overriding middle class and through
keyword-less implicit overrides; non-virtual name hiding and statics
(the shape of the 11 grpc false positives this item measured) get none.
Not checked: same-name overloads with differing signatures.

